  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nA cooler and wetter pattern is in store through much of the work \nweek into next weekend. Moist southerly to southwesterly flow aloft \nalong with inverted surface troughing brings favorable moisture and \nlift for daily scattered thunderstorm development across much of the \nregion. Cooler temperatures begin for most on Tuesday where highs \nrange from the 80s to low 90s which will persist through the work \nweek. Coverage of showers and storms begin to significantly increase \n(> 60%) for areas out west (southeast New Mexico and far West Texas) \nas upper-level troughing pulls in more moisture from northwest \nMexico. Areas elsewhere should still expect isolated to scattered \nrain/storms (20-50%). Ensembles continue to signal PWATs reaching \nabove the 90th climatological percentile, indicating the threat of \nheavy rainfall and flash flooding especially for portions of far \nWest Texas and southeast New Mexico. Upper-level troughing will \npersist late work week bringing more potential rounds of heavy \nrainfall for previously mentioned areas. Rainfall amounts, timing, \nand impacts are uncertain at the moment. We will continue to monitor \ntrends closely. \n\nLamberson",
